Masr Delivery — Java Capstone Project

Part A — The Business Domain
A.1 Restaurants
The platform carries restaurants. Every restaurant has an identifier that is unique
platform-wide, a display name, a district it operates from (for example Maadi, Dokki,
Faisal, Nasr City, Heliopolis), one or more cuisine categories, an average rating
between 0.0 and 5.0, and an open/closed status.
Each restaurant offers menu items. A menu item has an identifier unique within its
restaurant, a name, a price in EGP, a category, a preparation time in minutes, and an
availability flag.
Menu items are not all the same kind of thing, and they must not be priced the
same way. The platform supports at least three kinds:
• A standard item is sold at its listed price.
• A combo bundles several items together and is sold at a discount off the sum
of its parts.
• A weighted item (grilled meats, for example) is priced per kilogram, and the
customer orders a quantity in kilograms rather than a count.
A new kind of item will certainly be added after launch. Adding it must not require
editing the code that prices an order.
A.2 Customers
A customer has a unique identifier, a name, an Egyptian mobile number, one or
more saved addresses (each with a district and a free-text detail line), a wallet
balance in EGP, and a loyalty tier.
There are three loyalty tiers, and a customer’s tier is derived from their
completed order count, never set by hand:
Tier Completed orders Benefit
Bronze 0 – 9 none
Silver 10 – 29 10% off the delivery fee
Gold 30 or more delivery fee waived entirely

A.3 Riders
A rider has a unique identifier, a name, a vehicle type (motorcycle, bicycle, or car), a
current district, an availability status, and a running count of completed deliveries.
A rider may hold at most one active order at a time. This rule is absolute and is
the single most important correctness property of your system.
A.4 Orders
An order links one customer, one restaurant, one delivery address, and one or more
line items (each a menu item plus a quantity). It records the moment it was placed,
and it moves through a lifecycle:
PLACED → ACCEPTED → PREPARING → READY → ASSIGNED → OUT_FOR_DELIVERY →
DELIVERED
At any point before OUT_FOR_DELIVERY, an order may instead move to CANCELLED.
Illegal transitions must be rejected, not silently ignored. An order cannot jump
from PLACED to DELIVERED. A DELIVERED order cannot be cancelled. A CANCELLED
order is final.

Part B — Pricing Rules
Every order total must be computed by these rules, in this order:
1. Subtotal — the sum of every line item. Standard items: price × count.
Combos: the bundle price × count. Weighted items: price per kg × kilograms
ordered.
2. Delivery fee — 15 EGP base, plus 3 EGP for every kilometre beyond the first
3 km between the restaurant’s district and the delivery district. Then the
loyalty benefit from A.2 is applied.
3. Service fee — 10% of the subtotal, rounded to the nearest piastre.
4. Promotion — at most one promotion code per order, applied to the subtotal
only (never to fees).
5. Total — subtotal + delivery fee + service fee − promotion discount. A total
may never be negative.
The platform supports several promotion types and will add more: a percentage off
the subtotal with a maximum discount cap, a fixed amount off, and free delivery. A
promotion may carry conditions — a minimum subtotal, an expiry date, a restriction
to one district, or a restriction to first-time customers.

Worked example. A customer in Faisal orders from a restaurant in Maadi, 12 km
away. Subtotal is 240.00 EGP. The customer is Bronze. Promotion NILE20 gives 20%
off the subtotal, capped at 50 EGP.
• Delivery fee: 15 + (12 − 3) × 3 = 42.00 EGP
• Service fee: 240.00 × 0.10 = 24.00 EGP
• Promotion: 240.00 × 0.20 = 48.00, which is under the 50.00 cap, so 48.00
EGP
• Total: 240.00 + 42.00 + 24.00 − 48.00 = 258.00 EGP
Your program must produce exactly this total for exactly this input. It will be tested.
A note on money. Think carefully about how you represent EGP amounts.
Currency and binary floating point have a long and unhappy history
together. Whatever you choose, be ready to defend it.

Part C — How Data Must Be Organised
This section states performance and behaviour requirements. Choosing the data
structures that satisfy them is your job, and each choice must be justified in your
design document.
1. Looking up any restaurant, customer, rider, or order by its identifier must
not require scanning every record.
2. A restaurant’s menu must be displayed in the order the items were added,
consistently, on every run.
3. The platform must be able to list every distinct cuisine category currently
offered, with no duplicates.
4. Restaurants must be listable sorted by rating, highest first, with ties
broken alphabetically by name.

5. Orders that are READY but not yet assigned must be held so that the longest-
waiting order is dispatched first — except that orders from Gold

customers jump ahead of all Bronze and Silver orders regardless of waiting
time.
6. Each customer’s last five searches must be retrievable, newest first, without
keeping the whole history.
7. Two records describing the same real-world entity must never appear twice
in any collection. Decide what “the same entity” means for each type and
make your objects behave accordingly — including when they are used as
keys or stored in sets.
8. Any collection exposed outside the object that owns it must not be modifiable
by the caller.

Part D — Reporting and Analytics
We need the following reports. Each must be produced from the live data using the
modern Java toolkit — declarative pipelines over data, not hand-rolled
accumulation loops.
1. Total revenue for a given date range.
2. The top five restaurants by revenue, for a given month.
3. Average order value per district.
4. Every restaurant that has an average rating above 4.5 and at least twenty
completed orders.
5. A count of orders grouped by their current status.
6. Each rider’s completed deliveries and their average delivery duration, sorted
by deliveries descending.
7. The most frequently ordered menu item across the whole platform — noting
that when no orders exist at all, there is no such item, and the report must
express that absence honestly rather than inventing a value or crashing.
8. A customer’s full order history, newest first, with the total they have spent.
9. The peak ordering hour of the day across all orders.
10. Every customer who has not ordered in the last thirty days.
Dates and times throughout the system must use the modern date-and-time API. Do
not use the legacy classes.
You must also provide a search facility where the caller supplies the filtering
condition — by district, by cuisine, by minimum rating, by price ceiling, or any
combination — without the search code being modified for each new criterion.

Part F — When Things Go Wrong
The platform has failure conditions that Java’s built-in exceptions do not describe: a
restaurant that is closed, an item that is unavailable, a wallet without enough
balance, an order transition that is not legal, a promotion that has expired or does
not apply, a rider who is already busy, a stock shortage.
Design your own exception types for these. Organise them so that a caller can catch
one category of platform failure without catching every unrelated problem in the
JVM.
For each one you must decide: checked or unchecked? State the rule you applied,
and apply it consistently.

Part G — Design Decisions You Must Make and Defend
You have studied design patterns. This section does not tell you which ones to use.
It describes problems in this system whose shape should feel familiar. For each,
choose an approach, implement it, and justify it in your design document —
including what you rejected and why.
1. Pricing varies by promotion type, and new promotion types will be added
regularly. The order-total code must not grow a chain of type checks or a
switch that must be edited every time marketing invents something.
2. Building an order is a multi-step affair — customer, restaurant, address,
several line items, an optional promotion, optional delivery notes — and
most of those steps are optional. A constructor with nine parameters, most of
them null, is not acceptable.
3. Creating the right kind of menu item from a data file that contains a type
field should not scatter type-decision logic across the codebase.
4. When an order changes status, several unrelated things must happen:
the customer is notified, the rider dashboard updates, the audit log records it,
and statistics are recalculated. Adding a fourth reaction later must not
require modifying the order.
5. The platform configuration — fee rates, pool size, file paths — must be
loaded exactly once and be reachable from anywhere that needs it, without
being passed through every constructor in the system. Note that this one is
more contentious than it looks, and its interaction with Part E is a legitimate
thing to discuss.
6. A rider’s dispatch behaviour differs for motorcycle, bicycle, and car (range,
speed, and eligible order size). Adding an electric scooter later must not
mean editing existing rider code.

